1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,947
While you wait for an answer, our app says:
thinking, eleven seconds.

2
00:00:04,183 --> 00:00:06,513
Afterwards it folds into a receipt:

3
00:00:06,513 --> 00:00:09,708
thought for 22 seconds,
which is the whole wait.

4
00:00:10,028 --> 00:00:12,817
Open it, and the wait splits in two:

5
00:00:12,817 --> 00:00:17,389
only 13.5 seconds of thinking,
then 8.7 seconds of writing.

6
00:00:17,633 --> 00:00:18,892
Thinking about what?

7
00:00:19,212 --> 00:00:22,369
Three years ago,
no chat app showed you a model thinking.

8
00:00:22,689 --> 00:00:25,591
And why does ours show you a clock,
but never the thoughts?

9
00:00:27,133 --> 00:00:29,074
The idea is older than the apps.

10
00:00:29,394 --> 00:00:32,339
In January 2022, Google researchers showed

11
00:00:32,339 --> 00:00:37,736
that a model solves more problems
if you ask it to write out its steps first.

12
00:00:38,056 --> 00:00:39,592
They called it chain of thought.

13
00:00:39,912 --> 00:00:43,410
Months later, one sentence did it:
let's think step by step.

14
00:00:43,730 --> 00:00:45,309
It was a trick in the prompt.

15
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:48,705
In September 2024, OpenAI released o1,

16
00:00:48,705 --> 00:00:54,681
a model trained with reinforcement
learning to reason before it answers.

17
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:59,351
OpenAI chose not to show the raw thoughts,
citing user experience,

18
00:00:59,351 --> 00:01:02,909
competitive advantage,
and the chance to monitor them,

19
00:01:02,909 --> 00:01:06,072
and showed a summary written
by a model instead.

20
00:01:06,316 --> 00:01:07,745
Others chose differently.

21
00:01:08,065 --> 00:01:11,799
DeepSeek's R1 handed its reasoning text
to developers.

22
00:01:12,119 --> 00:01:15,959
Claude 3.7 showed its raw thinking,
as a research preview.

23
00:01:16,279 --> 00:01:19,335
From Claude 4, long thinking was summarized,

24
00:01:19,335 --> 00:01:22,530
and today's Claude API
returns only a summary.

25
00:01:22,766 --> 00:01:25,582
Today, thinking is a setting
you choose and pay for.

26
00:01:25,902 --> 00:01:28,078
OpenAI calls it reasoning effort.

27
00:01:28,398 --> 00:01:31,119
Our menu puts it next to the model choice,

28
00:01:31,119 --> 00:01:35,524
Luna, Terra and two others,
with three levels: low, medium and high.

29
00:01:36,867 --> 00:01:39,064
Here is what happens inside one call.

30
00:01:39,384 --> 00:01:43,032
A model writes one small piece of text
at a time, a token.

31
00:01:43,352 --> 00:01:46,190
A reasoning model first
writes tokens for itself.

32
00:01:46,510 --> 00:01:47,768
Those are its thoughts.

33
00:01:50,017 --> 00:01:52,790
OpenAI never sends those tokens back.

34
00:01:53,110 --> 00:01:57,676
They stay hidden, but they take up room,
and you pay for them at the output price.

35
00:01:57,917 --> 00:02:01,202
Only then does it write the answer,
our thirteen-box form.

36
00:02:01,522 --> 00:02:03,741
So our stopwatch can tell the two apart.

37
00:02:04,061 --> 00:02:05,341
Thinking is the time

38
00:02:05,341 --> 00:02:08,413
until the first character
of the answer arrives:

39
00:02:08,413 --> 00:02:12,189
the model reads the request,
then reasons in hidden tokens.

40
00:02:12,509 --> 00:02:15,350
Writing runs from there
until the model finishes;

41
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:16,626
then the checker runs.

42
00:02:16,867 --> 00:02:19,790
The effort setting sets how
much thinking the model may do.

43
00:02:20,110 --> 00:02:24,580
Even on the same setting,
the model decides how much it needs:

44
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:27,320
your greeting thought for 1.6 seconds,

45
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:30,926
a real question
about the student clinic for 13.5.

46
00:02:33,167 --> 00:02:34,589
If the thoughts are hidden,

47
00:02:34,589 --> 00:02:37,540
what exactly are you paying for,
and how would you know?

48
00:02:41,667 --> 00:02:44,261
Every response ends with a usage report,

49
00:02:44,261 --> 00:02:48,152
and it counts the reasoning tokens
inside the output tokens.

50
00:02:48,472 --> 00:02:49,565
Our server reads it,

51
00:02:49,565 --> 00:02:53,720
and the price list turns those counts
into the dollar figure on the receipt.

52
00:02:55,067 --> 00:02:56,838
Why show anything while you wait?

53
00:02:57,158 --> 00:02:59,220
The decision record puts it plainly:

54
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:03,344
ten to thirty seconds of waiting,
and nobody can tell what is happening.

55
00:03:03,664 --> 00:03:05,993
Most chat apps answer that by streaming:

56
00:03:05,993 --> 00:03:09,835
every few words travel
to your screen the moment they are written,

57
00:03:09,835 --> 00:03:12,048
over a pipe called server-sent events.

58
00:03:12,284 --> 00:03:15,484
Our server does receive
that stream from OpenAI.

59
00:03:15,804 --> 00:03:17,596
But it does not pass the words on.

60
00:03:17,916 --> 00:03:22,310
It reads the form as it fills,
notes which box the model is in,

61
00:03:22,310 --> 00:03:23,844
counts the characters,

62
00:03:23,844 --> 00:03:28,518
and sends the page only progress,
one small line of JSON at a time.

63
00:03:28,767 --> 00:03:31,775
That is what you watch:
thinking, eleven seconds.

64
00:03:32,095 --> 00:03:35,956
Writing the places to go,
320 characters so far.

65
00:03:36,276 --> 00:03:37,276
Checking.

66
00:03:37,407 --> 00:03:40,479
The page never sees a word
before the checker has.

67
00:03:41,817 --> 00:03:44,370
Why not stream the words,
the way Claude does,

68
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:46,702
and pull them back if the checker objects?

69
00:03:50,450 --> 00:03:52,583
Because a screen cannot unsay something.

70
00:03:52,903 --> 00:03:56,527
If a dose or a made-up phone
number flashes for two seconds,

71
00:03:56,527 --> 00:03:59,367
a worried parent may already
have a screenshot.

72
00:03:59,687 --> 00:04:03,357
So the decision record says:
stream the progress, not the text.

73
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:04,944
You asked for streaming.

74
00:04:05,264 --> 00:04:08,897
The agent, working
unattended, chose this kind on its own,

75
00:04:08,897 --> 00:04:13,094
and wrote it down as a proposal
that is still waiting for your yes.

76
00:04:14,433 --> 00:04:16,566
Progress streaming has one more trick.

77
00:04:16,886 --> 00:04:19,981
The form's first five boxes
are all about urgency,

78
00:04:19,981 --> 00:04:21,900
so the model writes them first.

79
00:04:22,150 --> 00:04:26,941
The moment those first boxes say emergency,
the server raises a flag,

80
00:04:26,941 --> 00:04:30,273
and the page shows
a warning we wrote ourselves:

81
00:04:30,273 --> 00:04:33,883
possibly life-threatening,
don't wait, call 911 now.

82
00:04:34,116 --> 00:04:37,865
In a real test,
the warning appeared at 3.5 seconds,

83
00:04:37,865 --> 00:04:40,964
and the checked answer at about 21 seconds.

84
00:04:41,284 --> 00:04:45,764
And since an emergency is never taken back,
that answer is bound to agree.

85
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:49,541
When the checked answer finally arrives,

86
00:04:49,541 --> 00:04:52,957
the page plays it out
in at most two and a half seconds:

87
00:04:52,957 --> 00:04:55,825
sentences type themselves,
lists drop in whole.

88
00:04:56,145 --> 00:04:57,468
It looks like streaming.

89
00:04:57,788 --> 00:05:00,412
It is a replay of text
that has already passed.

90
00:05:00,650 --> 00:05:03,637
Emergency answers skip the show
and appear at once.

91
00:05:03,957 --> 00:05:07,079
And the stop button really stops:
the page hangs up,

92
00:05:07,079 --> 00:05:10,442
and the server cancels the model
call it was paying for.

93
00:05:11,783 --> 00:05:16,284
On Wednesday morning, Boston time,
the streaming went in within twenty minutes.

94
00:05:16,604 --> 00:05:19,275
A few minutes later, a note for the menu:

95
00:05:19,275 --> 00:05:22,337
switching model or effort
starts a fresh cache,

96
00:05:22,337 --> 00:05:26,311
so on Terra that first answer costs
about five times as much.

97
00:05:26,550 --> 00:05:30,042
Much of the rest of the day went
to what reviews found:

98
00:05:30,042 --> 00:05:34,359
keeping the 911 warning
through stop, retry and dropped connections,

99
00:05:34,359 --> 00:05:37,914
and a test that fails
if any four consecutive characters

100
00:05:37,914 --> 00:05:42,230
of the model's text show up
in the progress lines before the result.

101
00:05:42,550 --> 00:05:47,478
That test is the proof that no words reach
the page before the checker has passed them.

102
00:05:48,817 --> 00:05:51,740
So what sits behind "thought
for 22 seconds"?

103
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:55,612
Claude's app also shows a timer
while it thinks, and,

104
00:05:55,612 --> 00:05:59,633
like ChatGPT, opens
onto a summary of the model's reasoning.

105
00:05:59,953 --> 00:06:01,600
Ours opens onto a receipt:

106
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:06,353
the timings, the sources, the check,
the judgment, the model and the price.

107
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:08,776
The reason is in the decision record.

108
00:06:09,096 --> 00:06:11,763
Raw reasoning
may contain diagnostic guesses,

109
00:06:11,763 --> 00:06:13,363
and nothing has checked it.

110
00:06:13,683 --> 00:06:17,694
In a medical product,
a guess on screen reads like a diagnosis.

111
00:06:19,033 --> 00:06:20,206
Where could this go?

112
00:06:20,526 --> 00:06:23,790
OpenAI can return a summary
of the hidden reasoning.

113
00:06:24,110 --> 00:06:26,301
But a summary is written by a model too,

114
00:06:26,301 --> 00:06:29,806
so it would have to pass the same checker
before anyone sees it.

115
00:06:30,126 --> 00:06:34,265
The progress line could name real steps,
like checking the clinic's hours.

116
00:06:34,585 --> 00:06:37,159
And the agent raised a developer-only switch

117
00:06:37,159 --> 00:06:39,790
to see unchecked drafts
while tuning prompts.

118
00:06:40,110 --> 00:06:41,284
It was not built.

119
00:06:41,533 --> 00:06:43,154
Three decisions wait for you.

120
00:06:43,474 --> 00:06:48,052
Progress-only streaming and the model menu
are written down as proposals;

121
00:06:48,052 --> 00:06:50,685
the developer switch, as an open question.

122
00:06:52,033 --> 00:06:54,102
So, thought for 22 seconds.

123
00:06:54,422 --> 00:06:58,042
First thinking:
hidden tokens the model writes for itself,

124
00:06:58,042 --> 00:06:59,414
billed but never sent.

125
00:06:59,734 --> 00:07:03,062
Then writing,
which our server watches box by box,

126
00:07:03,062 --> 00:07:05,324
passing on progress but not words.

127
00:07:05,644 --> 00:07:08,950
An early warning
when the first boxes say emergency.

128
00:07:09,270 --> 00:07:11,425
A replay once the checker has passed it.

129
00:07:11,745 --> 00:07:13,708
And a receipt instead of the thoughts.

130
00:07:13,950 --> 00:07:15,059
Three questions.

131
00:07:15,379 --> 00:07:17,854
What exactly does our stopwatch
call thinking?

132
00:07:18,174 --> 00:07:21,118
Why do the urgency boxes
come first in the form?

133
00:07:21,438 --> 00:07:24,616
And what would a reasoning summary need
before it could be shown?

134
00:07:24,866 --> 00:07:28,543
Next time, the page itself:
what we copied from Claude's app,

135
00:07:28,543 --> 00:07:31,074
what we refused, and what we could invent.

